### star-plan-executor/SKILL.md
--- 1
1. `EnterPlanMode` — except at involve `low`, where Step 4 takes the approval unasked and Claude Code's plan dialog waits for a keypress no hook can supply: draft EXEC_PLAN outside plan mode, everything else unchanged.
+++ 1
1. Ask the user to turn plan mode on. DSH ships no tool that enters it — plan mode is a session state the human selects, and `exit_plan_mode` fails when called outside it. If they decline, or the surface offers no plan mode, Step 4 takes its plain-text fallback; nothing else about it changes. At involve `low`, do not ask: Step 4 takes the approval unasked there, so EXEC_PLAN is drafted outside plan mode, everything else unchanged.
### star-plan-executor/SKILL.md
--- 1
**Before the call, run the design check.** Send `references/design_check.md` out for a blind read: one read-only `subagent` subagent (`subagent_type: Explore`) briefed with exactly three files — the EXEC_PLAN just written, the leaf sub-plan, and the root plan read at its §4 alone, the only root section the checklist admits as evidence (name the checklist's `_zh` twin when the plan's frontmatter says `language: zh`; the delegate never picks) — and the scope "ONLY these three files, and of the root plan only its §4. Do not rank, do not decide, do not run anything." It returns, per item: `item`, `verdict: pass | fail | unclear`, `evidence`, `fix`. The main agent re-opens the cited line for every `fail` it means to raise — an absence `fail` quotes nothing, so for those it re-reads the section the item belongs to — then puts at most five findings on the page **above** the call, one line each; a `fail` it cannot confirm is dropped. The check reports; this confirmation point decides, and nothing here stops the run. Where no delegate is available the main agent runs the checklist itself (conventions §6.1).
+++ 1
**Before the call, run the design check.** Send `references/design_check.md` out for a blind read: one read-only `subagent` briefed with exactly three files — the EXEC_PLAN just written, the leaf sub-plan, and the root plan read at its §4 alone, the only root section the checklist admits as evidence (name the checklist's `_zh` twin when the plan's frontmatter says `language: zh`; the delegate never picks) — and the scope "ONLY these three files, and of the root plan only its §4. Do not rank, do not decide, do not run anything." It returns, per item: `item`, `verdict: pass | fail | unclear`, `evidence`, `fix`. The main agent re-opens the cited line for every `fail` it means to raise — an absence `fail` quotes nothing, so for those it re-reads the section the item belongs to — then puts at most five findings on the page **above** the call, one line each; a `fail` it cannot confirm is dropped. The check reports; this confirmation point decides, and nothing here stops the run. Where no delegate is available the main agent runs the checklist itself (conventions §6.1).
### star-plan-executor/SKILL.md
--- 1
1. `exit_plan_mode` presenting EXEC_PLAN + the changes it will make: files to be written, commands to be run, where the STOP line falls, rough cost/runtime — and the divergence table, stated as "approving this plan also syncs these back into the sub-plan". Ask at the same confirmation point whether each verified step gets its own commit (recommended), naming any path that already carries uncommitted changes — those are never staged. Say what saying no costs: if a later step has to be restored, the only record of where each step started is the one this run keeps itself (`references/agent_dispatch_spec.md`). Where Step 3 set `branch: <run>` or `worktree: <path>`, those two questions ride this same confirmation point, worded as `references/branch_rules.md` states (conventions §11, §11.7). At `low` the call is not made: the same material goes in full into the reply text, the questions riding it take their recommended answers, the decisions record logs the approval as taken unasked, and item 2 follows without waiting.
+++ 1
1. `exit_plan_mode` presenting EXEC_PLAN + the changes it will make: files to be written, commands to be run, where the STOP line falls, rough cost/runtime — and the divergence table, stated as "approving this plan also syncs these back into the sub-plan". Ask at the same confirmation point whether each verified step gets its own commit (recommended), naming any path that already carries uncommitted changes — those are never staged. Say what saying no costs: if a later step has to be restored, the only record of where each step started is the one this run keeps itself (`references/agent_dispatch_spec.md`). Where Step 3 set `branch: <run>` or `worktree: <path>`, those two questions ride this same confirmation point, worded as `references/branch_rules.md` states (conventions §11, §11.7). At `low` the approval is not asked: the same material goes in full into the reply text, the questions riding it take their recommended answers, the decisions record logs the approval as taken unasked, and item 2 follows without waiting.
